  "text": "To find the value of \\( b \\) such that the polynomial \\( y^3 + 3y^2 + ay + b = 0 \\) has at least two distinct integer roots, we need to analyze the given polynomial \\( x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0 \\).\n\nFirst, let's assume that the polynomial \\( x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0 \\) has integer roots. Let's denote these roots by \\( p, q, r \\). By Vieta's formulas, we know:\n\n1. \\( p + q + r = -3 \\)\n2. \\( pq + qr + rp = a \\)\n3. \\( pqr = -7 \\)\n\nSince \\( p, q, r \\) are integers and their product is \\(-7\\), the possible sets of roots are \\((1, -1, -7)\\), \\((1, 1, -7)\\), \\((-1, -1, 7)\\), and \\((-1, 1, 7)\\).\n\nWe will check each set to find the value of \\( a \\) and then determine \\( b \\).\n\n**Case 1: Roots are \\( 1, -1, -7 \\)**\n\nHere, \\( p = 1 \\), \\( q = -1 \\), and \\( r = -7 \\).\n\nUsing Vieta's formulas:\n\\[ a = pq + qr + rp = (1)(-1) + (-1)(-7) + (-7)(1) = -1 + 7 - 7 = -1 \\]\n\\[ b = -pqr = -1 \\cdot (-1) \\cdot (-7) = -7 \\]\n\nSo, \\( a = -1 \\) and \\( b = -7 \\).\n\n**Case 2: Roots are \\( 1, 1, -7 \\)**\n\nHere, \\( p = 1 \\), \\( q = 1 \\), and \\( r = -7 \\).\n\nUsing Vieta's formulas:\n\\[ a = pq + qr + rp = (1)(1) + (1)(-7) + (-7)(1) = 1 - 7 - 7 = -15 \\]\n\\[ b = -pqr = -1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-7) = 7 \\]\n\nSo, \\( a = -15 \\) and \\( b = 7 \\).\n\n**Case 3: Roots are \\( -1, -1, 7 \\)**\n\nHere, \\( p = -1 \\), \\( q = -1 \\), and \\( r = 7 \\).\n\nUsing Vieta's formulas:\n\\[ a = pq + qr + rp = (-1)(-1) + (-1)(7) + (7)(-1) = 1 - 7 - 7 = -13 \\]\n\\[ b = -pqr = -(-1) \\cdot (-1) \\cdot 7 = -7 \\]\n\nSo, \\( a = -13 \\) and \\( b = -7 \\).\n\n**Case 4: Roots are \\( -1, 1, 7 \\)**\n\nHere, \\( p = -1 \\), \\( q = 1 \\), and \\( r = 7 \\).\n\nUsing Vieta's formulas:\n\\[ a = pq + qr + rp = (-1)(1) + (1)(7) + (7)(-1) = -1 + 7 - 7 = -1 \\]\n\\[ b = -pqr = -(-1) \\cdot 1 \\cdot 7 = 7 \\]\n\nSo, \\( a = -1 \\) and \\( b = 7 \\).\n\nFrom the above cases, we see that the values of \\( b \\) are \\( -7 \\) and \\( 7 \\). The problem asks for the value of \\( b \\) such that the polynomial \\( y^3 + 3y^2 + ay + b = 0 \\) has at least two distinct integer roots. Both values of \\( b \\) satisfy this condition.\n\nTherefore, the possible values of \\( b \\) are \\(\\boxed{-7}\\) and \\(\\boxed{7}\\).",
